; Leg PA -- BASELINE.  Host prefixes everything, block check 3.
;
; This is the 2x2's origin: prefixing ALL x block 3.  The other three legs
; each change one thing.
;
;   leg   host prefixing   host block
;   PA    all              3            <- this file
;   PC    cautious         3
;   BK    all              1
;   BX    cautious         1
;
; "set prefixing all" is stated rather than assumed.  The first round left
; it at whatever the Mac's default is and the whole comparison then rested
; on an unknown -- which is how the first round produced a 13% prediction
; for a change that could not do anything.  Pin it.
;
; PREFIXING IS A SENDER-SIDE DECISION and this is the sending end, which is
; the correction the first round earned: ctlp[] is read only in bgetpkt()
; (ckcfns.c:1798) and getpkt() (2904), both packet BUILDERS.  Nothing in
; decode()/bdecode() consults it, because a bare control character decodes
; as itself.  So on a Victor RECEIVE the Victor's own setting cannot
; matter, and round 1's measured proof is that leg BK (Victor PX_CAU, zero
; resends) put 37,534 bytes on the wire against SS16v leg CA's 37,536.
;
; All four legs run the shipping CKERMITW.EXE for the same reason -- there
; is nothing left for a Victor-side control build to control.

set line /dev/tty.usbserial-ABBFKXM1
set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing all
set block 3
log packets s16aePA.pkt
send rcvpa.dat
statistics
